---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const SITE = "https://layr.dynshift.com/";
const NPM = "https://www.npmjs.com/package/@dynshift/layr";
const REPO = "https://github.com/nishanbhuinya/layr";
---

<Base
  title="Layr"
  description="Layr brings Flutter's layout vocabulary to React: Container, Row, Column, Stack and the rest as type-safe components with no dependencies. What it is for and what it deliberately leaves out."
>
  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="meta muted">Project · React + TypeScript · MIT · Version 1.0.2, December 2025</p>
    <h1>Layr</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Flutter's layout primitives as React components, so a layout reads the
      way it is drawn.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a class="btn" href={SITE}>layr.dynshift.com</a>
      <a href={NPM} rel="noopener">@dynshift/layr on npm</a>
      <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      React has no layout system of its own. In practice a developer chooses
      between raw CSS and flexbox, which is powerful but verbose and easy to get
      subtly wrong; utility class frameworks, which are fast to write and hard
      to read back; or a full component library, which brings buttons, forms and
      a theme along with the layout it was wanted for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Flutter took a different path years ago. Its layout is a small set of
      widgets with deterministic, documented rules: a <code>Container</code>
      defines bounds and decoration, a <code>Row</code> lays children out
      horizontally, a <code>Column</code> vertically, a <code>Stack</code>
      layers them. Anyone who has built a screen in Flutter can look at the
      widget tree and see the layout. Layr brings exactly that vocabulary to
      React, with the same names and, wherever React allows it, the same API.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in it</h2>
    <p>
      Layout only. <code>Container</code>, <code>Row</code>, <code>Column</code>,
      <code>Stack</code>, <code>Positioned</code>, <code>SizedBox</code>,
      <code>Padding</code> and <code>Centre</code>, plus the alignment and
      sizing enums Flutter developers already know, such as
      <code>MainAxisAlignment</code>. There are no buttons, no inputs, no
      theming system, and nothing that renders visible chrome of its own. The
      library composes with whatever component set a project already uses.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No dependencies.</strong> Plain React components, tree-shakeable,
        so a project ships only the primitives it imports.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>TypeScript first.</strong> Every prop is typed; a width given
        as a string where a number is expected is an editor error, not a
        runtime surprise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Predictable.</strong> Each primitive has one job and documented
        rules for how it sizes itself and its children, mirroring Flutter's
        layout algorithm rather than reinterpreting it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      Teams that work in both Flutter and React and want one mental model for
      layout; and React developers who would rather describe a layout as a
      tree of intentions than as a cascade of properties. It is not a
      replacement for a design system. It is the layer underneath one.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting it</h2>
    <p>
      Install <code>@dynshift/layr</code> from npm. The
      <a href={SITE}>Layr site</a> has the documentation, a migration guide for
      Flutter developers, and a playground where each primitive can be tried
      with live code. The source, issues and pull requests are on
      <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .meta {
    margin-bottom: 1.2em;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px 24px;
    margin: 1.4em 0 1em;
  }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 11px 18px;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--ground);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .btn:hover {
    background: #fff;
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
